## How it works

The arms, the population, the unit guard and the window are those of
[`get_ab_test`](/docs/tools/get-ab-test): `diff` is resolved per path, an
`assignment` table gives the intention-to-treat population (silent assigned paths
count with no transition), and `horizon` keeps each path's first days after
`assigned_at`, leaving paths whose window had not closed out (`immature_paths`).
Each path's transitions are counted once, with the synthetic `path_start` and
`path_end` events of the graph, and every edge is compared as a per-path quantity:

| `edge_weight` | Per-path quantity | Arm value | Test |
|---|---|---|---|
| `unique_paths` | 1 if the path has the transition | share of paths | pooled z-test, Newcombe interval |
| `avg_per_path` | the transition's count | mean count per path | Welch's t-test |
| `proba_out` | count, over the path's transitions out of `source` | ratio of sums | joint path bootstrap |
| `proba_in` | count, over the path's transitions into `target` | ratio of sums | joint path bootstrap |
| `share_of_total` | count, over the path's transitions | ratio of sums | joint path bootstrap |

On the stream's paths an arm's value is the number
[`transition_graph_data`](/docs/widgets/transition-graph) draws for that arm with
the same `edge_weight` (for `unique_paths` the graph counts paths; the row gives
their share, and `group1_edge_paths` / `group2_edge_paths` hold the counts). With an
`assignment` table the per-path weights have the table's denominators, silent paths
included, so they differ from the graph's. `"count"` raises, because a raw count
grows with the arm's size (use `"avg_per_path"`); the time weights (`time_median`,
`time_q95`) are not tested in this release.

The three ratio weights divide by behaviour the treatment may change: a treatment
that sends more users to `cart` changes who leaves `cart`, and so `proba_out` from
it, without changing what any one user does there. Their rows carry
`conditional=True` and their reading says so; they compare transitions, not the
arms. `unique_paths` and `avg_per_path` are per-path quantities over every path of
the arm, so their deltas are effects of the assignment.

### The family and the verdict

An edge is tested when its pooled support reaches `min_support`: paths with the
transition, and for `unique_paths` paths without it too. The rest are reported with
their estimates, `verdict="insufficient_data"`, and are not in the family. The
p-values of the tested edges are adjusted together (`correction`,
Benjamini-Hochberg by default: among the edges called significant, the share of
false discoveries is at most about `1 - ci_level` on average), the verdict reads
only the adjusted p-value, and `attrs["n_tested"]` and `correction_family` say what
the family was. The intervals are per edge and not simultaneous; only
`correction="bonferroni"` adds simultaneous ones (`delta_ci_low_adj`,
`delta_ci_high_adj`, at `1 - alpha / n_tested`): Newcombe or Welch for the per-path
weights, and `delta ± z · delta_se` for the bootstrapped ratios, because a
percentile that far in the tail is beyond what `n_bootstrap` replicates resolve (it
is their minimum or maximum); they exclude 0 exactly when `p_value_adjusted` is
below alpha.

The discoveries are selected for a large estimate, so their deltas and intervals
sit further from 0 than the true effects on average (the winner's curse): size a
confirming test on a smaller effect. Under `horizon`, `path_end` is the last event
inside each path's window, so `X -> path_end` means the window closed after `X`,
not that the user left.

The family is every edge with enough support, found after looking at the data: a
screen. Its discoveries are where the arms differ, not decisions. To confirm one,
declare it as the metric of a new test (for example `matches_pattern` on the two
events). Declaring a few edges before the test with `edges=[(source, target),
...]` makes them the whole family, and their rows the only ones returned.

Simulated under a true null (20 seeds of the synthetic A/A data, about 37 tested
edges each), no run gave a discovery at `unique_paths`, `avg_per_path`, `proba_out`
or `share_of_total`, and the raw p-values of `unique_paths`, `avg_per_path` and
`proba_out` were uniform (share below 0.05: 0.041, 0.043 and 0.051).

### Reading a row

The `reading` column follows `get_ab_test`'s rules and adds the screen. On
`load_ab_test()` with `diff=("exp_group", "treatment", "control")`, 90 of the 92
edges are tested:

- **increase / decrease** — `"over the whole observation period: per-comparison
  interval [-37.36, -33.66] pp excludes 0 (p < 0.0001 after bh); relative -55 %;
  screen result (bh over 90 tested edges): a hypothesis to confirm as the declared
  metric of a new test, not a decision"` (`product_view -> add_to_cart`).
- **inconclusive** — `"...: per-comparison interval [-3.76, +0.12] pp includes 0
  (p 0.30 after bh); effects outside it are unlikely; the design detects 4.26 pp at
  alpha 0.0005556 — ...; screen: bh over 90 tested edges"`. The `mde` is computed at
  `alpha / n_tested`, a conservative level for the screen.
- **insufficient_data** — `"...: pooled support 8 < min_support 10; estimate shown
  descriptively; not tested: outside the screen's family (bh over 90 tested
  edges)"`.
- a ratio weight adds `conditional on post-treatment behaviour: a share of the
  transitions out of 'product_view' (4593 of 4828 and 4654 of 4905 paths have one),
  so it also moves when the treatment changes who reaches 'product_view'; compares
  those transitions, not the arms` (`edge_weight="proba_out"`).

### Highlighting the graph

`transition_graph(diff=..., ab_transitions=frame)` draws the frame over the diff
graph: significant edges in their sign's colour (red where group 1 is higher, blue
where it is lower) at full opacity, inconclusive edges thin and grey, untested ones
grey and dashed, and edges without a row in the frame (filtered out, or outside its
window) faintest. The edge tooltip shows the test's values — for `unique_paths` the
arms' shares in %, not the graph's path counts, whose difference can even have the
other sign when the arms differ in size — the delta, its interval, the adjusted
p-value and the verdict; the legend names the correction, the family size, ITT and
the horizon. A significant edge stays visible whatever the graph's edge filter. The
frame must have the graph's `diff` (segment form or id lists), `edge_weight` and
`path_col`, and `path_pattern` is refused; when the sidebar switches to another
weight, diff or path column, the overlay steps aside and says so. A filtered frame
(`frame[frame["verdict"] != "inconclusive"]`) is accepted, and the legend says it was
filtered.

